# Astronomical Poetry Dating

The silver moon is set;
The Pleiades are gone;
Half the long night is spent, and yet
I lie alone.

This is the John Merivale translation of fragment 58 by Sappho, often known as the Midnight Poem. In 2016, scholars combined the information in the poem, the location of the poet, and astronomical calculations to provide an estimate of when the poem was written (Cuntz et al, 2016). That particular estimate was 570 BC. This project reproduces the calculation using current data science tools. It also explores how sensitive the result is to parameter selection, the details of which are the result of deep scholarship. Indeed, scholars point out the poem may not even be by Sappho. (See ##Authorship Debate.)

The calculation assumes the report of seeing the constellation at a particular time is factual, rather than fictional. It also places Sappho at Myteline.


